    "result_text": "All 35 tests pass with `BUILD SUCCESS`.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the email-uniqueness rule in `addOwner` of `OwnerRestControllerV1.java` (the `POST /api/owners` handler).\n\nThe change adds a check immediately after the existing telephone-uniqueness check:\n\n```java\nString normalizedEmail = owner.getEmail();\nif (normalizedEmail != null) {\n    boolean emailInUse = this.clinicService.findAllOwners().stream()\n        .map(Owner::getEmail)\n        .filter(java.util.Objects::nonNull)\n        .map(existing -> existing.toLowerCase(java.util.Locale.ROOT))\n        .anyMatch(normalizedEmail::equals);\n    if (emailInUse) {\n        throw new ResponseStatusException(HttpStatus.CONFLICT,\n            \"An owner with this email address already exists\");\n    }\n}\n```\n\nKey points, consistent with the existing conventions in this method:\n- The new owner's email is already lower-cased at this point (via `normalizeEmail` earlier in the method), and each existing owner's email is lower-cased before comparison, so the match is on the **lower-cased** email as the spec requires.\n- A `null`/absent email is skipped \u2014 email is an optional field, so owners without an email never conflict with each other.\n- On a duplicate it throws `ResponseStatusException(HttpStatus.CONFLICT, \u2026)`, yielding a **409**, exactly mirroring the adjacent telephone-conflict rule.\n\nThe full suite (`./mvnw test`) is green: 35 run, 0 failures, 0 errors.",
